{
 "ep": 23,
 "total": 140,
 "title": "Connection Limits and max_connections",
 "phase": "Installation & Administration",
 "lines": [
  {
   "say": "Why raising max_connections can make things worse.",
   "cap": "Why *more connections* can make things *worse.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "This is episode twenty three of the PostgreSQL DBA series. Today: connection slots, the reserved ones, and why bigger is not always better.",
   "cap": "Episode 23: *connection slots* and their *cost.*",
   "cue": "reserved"
  },
  {
   "say": "max_connections sets the maximum number of concurrent connections to the database server.",
   "cap": "*max_connections* = max *concurrent* connections.",
   "cue": "concurrent"
  },
  {
   "say": "The default is typically one hundred, and it can only be set at server start.",
   "cap": "Default *100.* Set at *server start* only.",
   "cue": "start"
  },
  {
   "say": "Why does that matter? PostgreSQL uses a process per user model.",
   "cap": "A *process per user* model.",
   "cue": "process"
  },
  {
   "say": "The postmaster listens for connections, and spawns a new backend process for each one.",
   "cap": "postmaster *spawns a backend* per connection.",
   "cue": "backend"
  },
  {
   "say": "So every single connection costs a whole server process.",
   "cap": "1 connection = *1 server process.*",
   "cue": "process"
  },
  {
   "say": "And not every slot is for your app. superuser_reserved_connections keeps three slots for superusers by default.",
   "cap": "*3 slots* reserved for *superusers.*",
   "cue": "three"
  },
  {
   "say": "reserved_connections can keep more slots for roles with pg_use_reserved_connections. Its default is zero.",
   "cap": "*reserved_connections:* default *0.*",
   "cue": "zero"
  },
  {
   "say": "So with the defaults, normal roles get ninety seven slots.",
   "cap": "100 minus 3 minus 0 = *97* for normal roles.",
   "cue": "ninety"
  },
  {
   "say": "Let's prove it. In a lab, we set max_connections to six, and restarted.",
   "cap": "Lab: *max_connections = 6.*",
   "cue": "six"
  },
  {
   "say": "Three app_user sessions are open. A fourth app_user connection tries to log in.",
   "cap": "3 sessions open. A *4th* tries.",
   "cue": "fourth"
  },
  {
   "say": "Refused. The error, output trimmed, says the remaining slots are reserved for superusers.",
   "cap": "*Refused:* slots reserved for *SUPERUSER.*",
   "cue": "reserved"
  },
  {
   "say": "Fill all six, and even a superuser gets: sorry, too many clients already.",
   "cap": "All full: *sorry, too many clients already.*",
   "cue": "sorry"
  },
  {
   "say": "So why not just set it to five thousand?",
   "cap": "Why not set it to *5000?*",
   "cue": "thousand"
  },
  {
   "say": "Each backend has its own working memory. work_mem applies per sort or hash operation, not per server.",
   "cap": "*work_mem* is per *operation,* not per server.",
   "cue": "operation"
  },
  {
   "say": "The docs warn that total memory used could be many times work_mem, when many sessions do this at once.",
   "cap": "Total can be *many times work_mem.*",
   "cue": "many"
  },
  {
   "say": "The docs also count one semaphore per allowed connection, so the kernel must allow enough.",
   "cap": "*1 semaphore* per allowed connection.",
   "cue": "semaphore"
  },
  {
   "say": "And on a standby, max_connections must be the same or higher than on the primary.",
   "cap": "Standby: *same or higher* than primary.",
   "cue": "standby"
  },
  {
   "say": "So measure before you change anything. Count client backends in pg_stat_activity.",
   "cap": "Measure first: *pg_stat_activity.*",
   "cue": "pg_stat_activity"
  },
  {
   "say": "Here, four are in use, including our own session, out of one hundred.",
   "cap": "*4 used* out of *100.*",
   "cue": "four"
  },
  {
   "say": "Common mistake: raising the limit every time you see too many clients.",
   "cap": "Mistake: *raise the limit* every time.",
   "cue": "mistake"
  },
  {
   "say": "A common practice is to keep max_connections modest, and put a connection pooler in front.",
   "cap": "Common practice: modest limit + *a pooler.*",
   "cue": "pooler"
  },
  {
   "say": "Recap: one process per connection, and three slots kept for superusers.",
   "cap": "Recap: *1 process each,* *3 reserved.*",
   "cue": "three"
  },
  {
   "say": "Memory grows with sessions. Measure first, then pool.",
   "cap": "*Memory grows.* Measure, *then pool.*",
   "cue": "pool"
  },
  {
   "say": "Next up: connection pooling with PgBouncer. Follow so you don't miss it, and send this to a friend who's learning PostgreSQL.",
   "cap": "Next up: *PgBouncer.* *Follow* and share."
  }
 ],
